GHthrift Comprehensive Plan
============================

Version 1.0 – October 2026
Prepared for: [Your Name/Business]

---
## 1. Executive Summary
GHthrift is an online marketplace focused on selling curated thrift dresses in Ghana. The platform will be available as a responsive website (mobile‑first) with optional progressive web app (PWA) and future native companion app. The business model centers on the founder sourcing dresses personally and handling sales, payments, and logistics. This document covers the full lifecycle from discovery and system design through development, launch, and marketing.

---
## 2. Phase A – Discovery & Requirements
### 2.1 Core Value Proposition
- Curated Ghana‑sourced and imported thrift dresses at affordable prices.
- Sustainable fashion alternative with unique, one‑of‑a‑kind pieces.
- Reliable, secure shopping experience with local payment options.

### 2.2 Primary Personas
| Persona | Age | Motivations | Pain Points |
|---------|-----|-------------|-------------|
| Urban Fashion‑Conscious | 18‑35 | Stay trendy, express individuality, budget‑friendly luxury. | Limited selection of unique pieces in local malls.
Budget‑Shoppers | 16‑30 | Maximize wardrobe variety for minimal cost. | Concerns about quality and authenticity.
Eco‑Conscious | 20‑40 | Reduce fashion waste, support circular economy. | Difficulty finding trustworthy thrift platforms.

### 2.3 Minimum Viable Feature Set (MVP)
- Product listings with photos, descriptions, sizes, colors, prices.
- Advanced search & filtering (size, color, price range, style/occasion).
- User accounts (register/login, wish list, order history).
- Secure checkout with Ghana Mobile Money (MTN, Airtel) and card payments (Paystack/Flutterwave).
- Order tracking (status: pending → paid → shipped → delivered).
- Simple admin dashboard (add/edit items, view orders, export sales).
- Basic contact/chat with seller (optional).

### 2.4 Tech‑Stack Shortlist
#### Option 1 – Full‑Stack JavaScript (quick prototype)
- **Frontend**: Next.js (React) – SSR for SEO, responsive out‑of‑the‑box.
- **Backend**: Node.js + Express (or NestJS) – lightweight APIs.
- **Database**: PostgreSQL (via Supabase or ElephantSQL).
- **Auth**: Supabase Auth or Auth0.
- **Payments**: Paystack SDK (supports Mobile Money & cards) + Flutterwave optional.
- **Storage**: Supabase Storage (S3‑compatible) or Cloudinary for dress photos.
- **Hosting**: Vercel (frontend) + Render/Railway (backend) or full‑stack on Supabase.

#### Option 2 – PHP Laravel (robust, common in Ghana)
- **Frontend**: Laravel Blade + Alpine.js, or a separate React SPA talking to Laravel API.
- **Backend**: Laravel (PHP) – elegant Eloquent ORM, built‑in auth, API resources.
- **Database**: MySQL/PostgreSQL via Laravel’s database layer.
- **Auth**: Laravel Breeze / Sanctum.
- **Payments**: Paystack/Flutterwave PHP SDKs.
- **Storage**: Laravel’s built‑in S3 (Spatie/Flysystem) or Cloudinary.
- **Hosting**: Shared cPanel for MVP → DigitalOcean droplet with LEMP + Laravel Forge for scale.

### 2.5 Web‑First vs App‑First
- **Start with a responsive website** (mobile‑first). >80% of Ghanaian internet users browse on Android phones.
- **PWA** can be added later for app‑like experience without separate native build.
- **Native app** (React Native or Flutter) considered after achieving product‑market fit.

---
## 3. Phase B – Sourcing & Inventory Model (High‑Level)
| Aspect | Action |
|--------|--------|
| **Acquisition** | Source from Kantamanto Market (Accra), Goodwill bins, direct-from‑owner sales. Establish quality‑check rubric (fabric condition, stains, size accuracy). |
| **Cataloguing** | Use a Google Sheet or lightweight DB with columns: Item ID, Source, Date Acquired, Price Paid, Size, Color, Style, Condition, Photos (URLs), Status (Available/Sold). |
| **Photography** | Natural‑light studio or clean backdrop. 3‑5 photos per dress: front, back, detail (fabric pattern), size label, close‑up of any flaws. |
| **Pricing Formula** | `Retail Price = Cost × 2.5–3` (adjust after competitor research on Jumia, Tonaton, Facebook groups). |
| **Inventory Sync** | When the platform is built, import sheet data or enter items directly so the website reflects real‑time availability. |

---
## 4. Phase C – System Design & Architecture
### 4.1 Core Data Model (Entities)
- **User**: `id, name, email, phone, password_hash, wishlist (IDs), address(es), created_at`.
- **Dress/Item**: `id, title, description, price, size, color, style, condition, photos (array of URLs), category, created_at, seller_id (for future marketplace)`.
- **Order**: `id, user_id, item_id (or bundle), quantity, total_price, payment_method, transaction_id, status (pending/paid/shipped/delivered), shipped_at, delivered_at, created_at`.
- **Payment**: `id, order_id, amount, provider (paystack/flutterwave), provider_ref, status, captured_at`.
- **Delivery/Logistics**: `id, order_id, partner_name, tracking_number, status, notes, delivered_at`.

### 4.2 Key User Flows
1. **Browse → Search/Filter → Product Page → Add to Cart → Checkout → Payment → Order Confirmation**.
2. **Admin**: List/manage items, view orders, update status (pending → paid → shipped → delivered), export CSV.
3. **Post‑Purchase**: Customer receives SMS/USSD confirmation; partner tracking updates; after delivery, request review/rating.

### 4.3 UI/UX Priorities (Ghana Context)
- **Mobile‑first** design – large touch targets, thumb‑friendly navigation.
- **Fast loading** – compress images (WebP, appropriate dimensions), lazy‑load.
- **Size guide** – include body measurements (cm) because Ghanaian sizing can differ from US/EU.
- **Trust signals** – verified seller badge, secure payment icons, clear return/refund policy.
- **Local language support** – optionally include Twi or Ga labels for key terms, but keep UI in English.

### 4.4 Detailed Tech‑Stack Comparison
| Layer | Option 1 (Node/React) | Option 2 (Laravel) |
|-------|----------------------|--------------------|
| **Frontend** | Next.js (SSR, SEO friendly) or CRA. | Blade templates or React via API. |
| **Backend** | Node + Express (REST) or tRPC for type‑safe calls. | Laravel (Eloquent, built‑in auth, API resources). |
| **Database** | PostgreSQL (Supabase or ElephantSQL). | MySQL/PostgreSQL via Laravel’s DB layer. |
| **Auth** | Supabase Auth / Auth0. | Laravel Breeze / Sanctum. |
| **Payments** | Paystack JS SDK, Flutterwave. | Paystack/Flutterwave PHP SDKs. |
| **Storage** | Supabase Storage (S3‑compatible) or Cloudinary. | Laravel S3 (Spatie/Flysystem) or Cloudinary. |
| **Hosting** | Vercel (frontend) + Render/Railway (backend) **or** full Supabase project. | Shared cPanel for MVP → DigitalOcean + Laravel Forge. |
| **Admin UI** | React + AdminBro or Strapi headless CMS. | Laravel Nova or Filament (drag‑and‑drop). |

